// PRIMEMET — Prisma schema, Milestone 1 (database schema foundation).
//
// Scope: architecture document §28.1 "M1 — Schema" — migrations 1–3, 6, 14, 15, 18, 19.
// Everything here is transcribed from docs/PRIMEMET_STAGE_2_ARCHITECTURE.md:
//   §5.1  conventions (uuid PKs, timestamptz, numeric precision, no float)
//   §5.2  enumerations
//   §5.3  Group A — identity, organisation and access
//   §8.3  Group C — catalogue and configurable composition
//   §8.4  Group D — parties (profiles and standing listings only; lots are M6)
//   §8.7  Group G — documents, pricing rule sets, audit, migration runs
//   §15.5 tax tables + D7 (invoice_role is never NULL)
//   §8.8  location_states — the only geography table M1 needs, because tax_rules references it
//
// Deliberately absent: enquiries, supplier quotes, buyer quotes, lots, inspections, approvals,
// dispatch, rejection, dispute, settlement, orders, invoices, payments, contact sharing, the rest of
// the geography tree, SEO tables, stock positions/movements. Those are M3–M9 (migration 4, 5, 7–13,
// 16, 17, 20). Their enums ARE declared here because §24.2 puts every enum in migration 1 and an
// enum value is append-only (§5.1).
//
// Constraint classes Prisma cannot express are applied by hand in
//   prisma/migrations/20261031000100_m1_constraints/migration.sql
// — CHECK constraints, partial unique indexes, the btree_gist EXCLUDE constraint, and Prisma's own
// index-name normalisation. See prisma/README.md.
//
// No DATABASE_URL is required to build or run the application. Prisma tooling reads it; the Next.js
// runtime does not (src/lib/db.ts is lazy and src/app/api/health/route.ts stays "not_configured").

generator client {
  provider = "prisma-client-js"
}

datasource db {
  provider = "postgresql"
  url      = env("DATABASE_URL")
}

